# DigiSwasthya Product Definition Document (PDD)

# Part 17 — Non-Functional Requirements

---

# Purpose

This section defines product-quality requirements that are not features themselves but constrain how DigiSwasthya must behave in use.

These are product requirements, not implementation prescriptions.

---

# 1. Performance

## Expectations

- Common list pages (orders, invoices, products) should feel responsive under normal business volumes.
- Search/filter on a business’s own dataset should return quickly enough for daily operations.
- Dashboard KPI cards for a single business should load without obstructing the primary work queue.
- Accept/reject/approve actions should confirm outcome promptly after commit.

## Volume Assumptions (Current Product Intent)

The product should comfortably support day-to-day pharmaceutical B2B activity for pharmacies and stockists with:

- Thousands of products per stockist catalogue
- Thousands of historical orders/invoices per business over time
- Concurrent staff users per business in small operational teams

Exact infrastructure sizing is out of scope here; the product must remain operable at realistic distributor/pharmacy scale.

## Degradation

When datasets are very large, the product may require filters/pagination/async export rather than failing or freezing.

---

# 2. Availability

- Core trading workflows (order, fulfilment status, invoicing, payment recording) are critical.
- Planned maintenance should be communicated via announcement/banner when possible.
- Temporary unavailability should surface clear unavailable state, not partial corrupted commits.
- After recovery, entity states must remain consistent with State Machines and Calculations.

---

# 3. Offline / Connectivity

## Current Product Position

DigiSwasthya is primarily online.

## Required Behaviour under Poor Connectivity

- Users must be able to recognise when an action did not complete.
- Critical create/approve actions must be retry-safe (no duplicate orders/payments on repeat submit).
- Users should be guided to verify whether an order/payment already exists after uncertain failure.
- Full offline ordering/inventory mode is out of current scope unless later explicitly added.

---

# 4. Security

## Access Security

- Authentication required for all business actions.
- Authorisation enforced by Permissions Matrix.
- Cross-business data isolation is mandatory.
- Admin access is read/governance oriented and must not enable silent financial manipulation.

## Credential Security

- Password reset and change flows must be protected.
- Suspended/removed users cannot authenticate into operational access.
- Security events (password change, suspension) generate mandatory notifications.

## Data Exposure

- Notifications, search, and errors must not leak another business’s private data.
- Verification documents visible only to owning business and platform admins.
- Customer-specific pricing visible only under Active connection rules.

---

# 5. Audit & Traceability

- Every significant business action records who, when, what changed, and why when required.
- Financial documents are immutable in substance after issue.
- Staff removal does not erase attribution.
- Admin governance actions are auditable.
- Exports of sensitive datasets are auditable.

---

# 6. Data Integrity & Consistency

- Single source of truth per entity.
- Inventory never negative.
- Outstanding never negative.
- Analytics cannot override source documents.
- Concurrent actions resolve to valid states, never hybrid illegal states.
- Historical readability preserved after discontinuations/disconnects/suspensions.

---

# 7. Reliability of Business Events vs Side Effects

- Primary business commit succeeds independently of notification delivery (default).
- Notification/analytics failures are secondary and recoverable.
- Settlement and inventory movements are atomic with respect to their own invariants.

---

# 8. Usability / Operational Simplicity

- Common tasks require minimum steps while preserving correctness.
- Error messages communicate business impact.
- Empty states guide next action (connect, add product, review pending).
- Role-specific homes prioritise queues over decorative metrics.

---

# 9. Accessibility

- Core flows usable with clear labels, readable contrast, and non-color-only status meaning.
- Status must not be communicated by color alone (text/status labels required).
- Touch targets and forms usable on mobile devices for operational roles.
- Exact WCAG target can be set by company standard; product must not depend on inaccessible-only cues.

---

# 10. Mobile

## Priority Mobile Users

- Delivery Boy execution
- Pharmacy receiving confirmations
- Quick order status / payment status checks
- Notifications triage

## Expectation

Operational journeys used in the field must be fully completable on mobile.

Heavy catalogue imports, complex bulk pricing, and large admin configuration may remain desktop-primary.

---

# 11. Scalability (Product Intent)

The domain model should extend toward:

- More businesses
- Larger catalogues
- Longer financial history
- More concurrent staff

without redesigning core entity meanings.

Future warehouses/branches must extend, not redefine, Order/Invoice/Payment meanings.

---

# 12. Localization & Time

- Money display consistent with INR business norms unless multi-currency later added.
- Date/time displayed in a consistent business timezone policy.
- Analytics period boundaries use the same timezone rules everywhere.
- Multilingual support may be phased; terminology must follow Product Glossary even if UI language expands.

---

# 13. Compliance Readiness (Product-Level)

Without claiming full legal certification in this document:

- Retain drug license / GST identity evidence for verification.
- Retain invoices, payments, credit notes, returns history.
- Do not supply expired batches as sellable goods.
- Support recall blocking for batches.
- Provide audit trails for significant actions.

Specific statutory formats/provisions should be added as explicit compliance features when required.

---

# 14. Privacy

- Personal staff contact data visible on need-to-know basis.
- Support access limited to ticket/governance needs.
- Message content visible to participants and authorised admins for safety/disputes only.
- Data retention/deletion requests cannot destroy statutory financial records.

---

# 15. Observability (Product Expectation)

Platform operators should be able to answer:

- What is pending verification?
- Where are settlement anomalies?
- Which businesses are suspended and why?
- What actions led to a disputed order/payment?

Via admin queues, audit logs, and support tools defined in capabilities.

---

# 16. Backup / Recovery (Product Expectation)

After recovery from outage:

- No loss of issued financial documents
- No loss of approved payments
- No inventory negative artefacts
- Users can reconcile by entity numbers (Order/Invoice/Payment numbers)

---

# 17. Future Integration Readiness

Architecture/product definitions should anticipate later integrations without implementing them now:

- Accounting exports
- Logistics partners
- Manufacturer feeds
- ABDM / health network hooks
- Payment gateway automation
- SMS/email providers

Integrations must not bypass Permissions, State Machines, or Calculations.

---

# 18. Quality Bars for Release Readiness (Product)

A release is product-incomplete if:

- Core journeys in Parts 8 lack corresponding acceptance criteria for critical paths
- Any forbidden state machine transition is possible
- Outstanding/inventory can go negative
- Cross-business data is visible illegally
- Expired batches can be delivered as sellable

---

# Completion Criteria

NFR expectations for performance intent, availability, security, audit, integrity, mobile ops, accessibility basics, and future-readiness are explicit.

